1️⃣ What is a Dockerfile? (Definition – Interview Ready)
🔑 Definition

A Dockerfile is a text file that contains a set of instructions used by Docker to build a container image in a layered, reproducible way.

🔑 In simple words

Dockerfile = Recipe

Docker image = Prepared dish

Docker container = Dish being served & eaten

🔑 MUST KNOW (4+ Years)

Dockerfile is not executed line-by-line at runtime

It is used only during image build

Each instruction creates a read-only layer

Layers are cached & reused

2️⃣ How Dockerfile Works Internally (IMPORTANT)

When you run:

docker build -t school-app .


Docker does:

Reads Dockerfile top to bottom

Executes each instruction

Creates an image layer

Caches that layer

Final image = stack of layers

👉 This is why instruction order matters.

3️⃣ General Dockerfile Syntax (Standard Format)

Most production Dockerfiles follow this pattern:

FROM <base-image>

WORKDIR <directory>

COPY <dependency-files>
RUN <install dependencies>

COPY <application-code>

EXPOSE <port>

CMD / ENTRYPOINT

🔑 MUST KNOW

FROM → mandatory

CMD or ENTRYPOINT → mandatory

Order affects:

Build speed

Image size

CI performance

4️⃣ Now YOUR Dockerfile – Line by Line (DEEP EXPLANATION)
🔹 FROM python:3.10-slim
What it means

Uses official Python Docker image

Python version = 3.10

OS = Debian slim

🔑 Why Python 3.10?

Stable

Long-term support

Compatible with Flask & pytest

Widely used in production

🔑 Why NOT latest?

❌ latest can break builds
✔ Fixed version ensures reproducibility

🔑 Why -slim?

Smaller image size

Faster pull

Reduced attack surface

Image	Size
python:3.10	~900 MB
python:3.10-slim	~120 MB

👉 Senior engineers always prefer slim

🔹 WORKDIR /app
What it does

Creates /app inside container

Sets it as default directory

All future commands run from /app

Equivalent to:
cd /app

🔑 Why is this important?

Avoids absolute paths

Cleaner Dockerfile

Prevents permission issues

⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE

❌ Not setting WORKDIR
✔ Leads to messy paths like /app/app/main.py

🔹 COPY requirements.txt .
What it does

Copies requirements.txt from host → container /app

🔑 WHY copy this FIRST? (VERY IMPORTANT)

This is called Docker Layer Caching Optimization.

Scenario:

You change app code

Dependencies stay same

Docker will:
✔ Reuse cached dependency layer
✔ Skip reinstalling packages
✔ Build faster

⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE

❌ Copy entire code first
❌ Then install dependencies
✔ This causes slow builds

🔹 RUN pip install --no-cache-dir -r requirements.txt
What it does

Installs Python dependencies inside image

🔑 Why --no-cache-dir?

Prevents pip cache storage

Reduces image size

Cleaner image

🔑 MUST KNOW

RUN executes during image build

Creates a permanent image layer

🔹 COPY . .
What it does

Copies entire project into /app

🔑 Why AFTER dependencies?

App code changes frequently

Dependencies don’t

This improves:
✔ Build speed
✔ CI efficiency

🔹 EXPOSE 5000
What it does

Documents that container listens on port 5000

Helps Docker & Kubernetes networking

🔑 MUST KNOW

EXPOSE does NOT publish port

-p in docker run publishes it

Interview Trap ❗

EXPOSE alone does NOT make app accessible

Correct answer:

Port mapping is done via docker run -p or Kubernetes Service.

🔹 CMD ["python", "app/main.py"]
What it does

Defines default command when container starts

🔑 CMD vs RUN (IMPORTANT)
RUN	CMD
Executes at build time	Executes at runtime
Creates image layer	Starts container
Cannot be overridden easily	Can be overridden
🔑 Why JSON array syntax?

Avoids shell issues

Better signal handling

Recommended by Docker

🔑 ENTRYPOINT vs CMD (GOOD TO KNOW)

CMD → default command

ENTRYPOINT → fixed command

We use CMD because:
✔ Simpler
✔ Flexible

5️⃣ How This Dockerfile Is Used in CI/CD
GitHub
  ↓
Jenkins
  ↓
docker build
  ↓
Docker image
  ↓
Artifact Registry
  ↓
GKE


👉 Same Dockerfile used everywhere

6️⃣ Real Interview Question & Answer
❓ Why is Dockerfile layered?

Answer:

“Docker images are built in layers to enable caching and faster rebuilds. Only layers affected by changes are rebuilt.”

7️⃣ Summary (What You MUST Remember)

🔑 Dockerfile = image blueprint
🔑 Order matters
🔑 Base image version must be fixed
🔑 Layer caching improves CI speed
🔑 Same image used in dev, CI & prod


